WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.460
 We normally start
in the dark

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:10.780
and then begin by carving out to bring
the audience's attention to 

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.490
one thing at a time.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.770
How do you give
physical concrete form

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:17.020
to something as abstract
and nebulous as music

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:20.110
or to an idea?

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:21.490
When I was at
school, my teachers

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.830
called me a jack of all
trades, master of none.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.860
The more paths I was offered,
the more I wanted to take.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.530
I wanted to read about
neuroscience and ecopsychology.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:33.460
I studied English literature,
then art, then stage design.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.100
It's quite unusual to make that
leap from classical theater

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.270
into pop music.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.810
And people are constantly
invited to specialize.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.930
And I'm here to teach
you to do the opposite.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:51.490
This class is for those who want
to turn what is in their mind

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.600
into something they
can hold in their hand,

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.690
whether it's within the
field you currently work

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:59.800
or specifically within
the field of design.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.500
Iterate, iterate,
iterate, even when

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:04.239
you think you've
arrived at something.

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:07.720
And you might even
just infuriate yourself

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.370
into having to come
up with a new idea.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.502
So many artists,
designers, makers--

00:01:12.502 --> 00:01:13.960
once you peel away
the surface, you

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.940
realize that they're
making in order

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:19.120
to conquer a sense of loss.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.130
And certainly, in
my experience, I

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:25.960
made a decision to throw myself
into my work in my early 20s

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.540
because of a loss that
happened in my life.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:33.160
That energy of creation
as a reaction against loss

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.130
is incredibly powerful.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.800
You have never
researched enough.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.770
Your life will be an
endless, and, I hope,

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:45.890
fruitful and happy journey
of not knowing enough.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.590
If you're choosing to watch
this class because you want

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:51.350
to keep expanding the
paths along which you walk,

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:54.130
you're in good company.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.650
I'm Es Devlin, and
this is my MasterClass.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

